  "explanation": {
    "summary": "In California, solar panels flood the grid at midday, so power plants have very little to do. At sunset, solar drops away just as people get home, and other plants must ramp up fast. Drawn over a day, this looks like a duck: a low belly at noon and a steep neck in the evening.",
    "methodology": "Hourly data for the California Independent System Operator (CAISO) from EIA Form 930 (EIA API, local time). Net load = demand minus solar minus wind. The belly is the average net load from 11 am to 3 pm. The neck is the evening peak (5 to 10 pm) minus the midday low. Both are monthly averages of daily values. Data on solar that had to be switched off is not in the EIA API.",
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "date": "2019-06-01",
        "title": "2019: The Duck Is Small",
        "description": "In June 2019 the grid still needed about 13,900 MW from other plants at midday, and the evening ramp was about 14,600 MW.",
        "significance": "Solar was large, but not yet dominant.",
        "normalized_time": 0.0543
      },
      {
        "date": "2023-07-01",
        "title": "July 2023: The Steepest Neck",
        "description": "The evening ramp reached 20,500 MW, the largest on the chart. Other plants had to add about 20 large power stations' worth of output within a few hours.",
        "significance": "This ramp is why California built so many batteries.",
        "normalized_time": 0.587
      },
      {
        "date": "2024-05-01",
        "title": "May 2024: The Deepest Belly",
        "description": "Midday net load fell to 5,700 MW, less than half its 2019 level, as solar covered most daytime demand.",
        "significance": "On spring days, solar alone almost runs the state at noon.",
        "normalized_time": 0.6957
      }
    ]
  }
}